WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.040
"The Side Effects Statins: Are They Worth It?"

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.840
In my last video, I tried
to quantify the benefits

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.800
of starting cholesterol-lowering
statin drugs,

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:14.720
but that’s only half
the equation.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.840
The number one reason people
may refuse to take statins

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.160
or stop taking them
is a concern about side effects.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:25.440
Statins are considered mandatory
for secondary prevention,

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.760
for those with known
cardiovascular disease

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.920
to prevent a second heart attack
or stroke,

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.360
but primary prevention,

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.280
trying to prevent
our first event,

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.520
is more of a gray area.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.000
A hundred adults
between the ages of 50 and 75

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.120
without known
cardiovascular disease

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.200
would have to be treated
with a statin for 2.5 years

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:48.240
to prevent one major
adverse cardiovascular event,

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:49.680
like a stroke
or a heart attack.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.440
That’s the upside.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:52.640
The downside,

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.920
if you treated 10,000
such people for a year,

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:58.000
the statins
would be expected to cause

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.000
fifteen cases
of muscle symptoms,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.160
eight cases
of liver dysfunction,

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.000
twelve cases
of kidney dysfunction,

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.240
and 14 more eye conditions,

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.520
while at the same time
preventing 19 heart attacks,

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.120
nine strokes,
and eight cardiovascular deaths.

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:14.960
So, approximately the same risk

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.280
of having a major side effect
or a major benefit,

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.160
but a benefit like preventing
a heart attack or death

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.600
is more important than avoiding
a case of blurred vision;

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:27.120
so, these reviewers suggest

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.040
cardiovascular benefits
of statins

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.280
outweigh adverse effects
in primary prevention.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:34.400
But wait.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.840
There were only 15 cases
of muscle symptoms

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:41.280
out of 10,000 people treated?

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:43.920
That is based on
the clinical trials

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.720
that found approximately
one in a thousand users

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:50.960
over a ten-year period
develops serious muscle disease.

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.280
Cases of muscle weakness
or muscle breakdown

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:54.720
are hard to miss,

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.440
whereas the incidence
of statin-induced muscle aches

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.320
and pains
is far more contentious.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.840
Such randomized
controlled trials

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.160
may seriously underestimate
adverse effects

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:05.440
like muscle pain,

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:08.960
since industry-sponsored trials
include these run-in periods

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:10.480
before the study even starts

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:13.920
to exclude people
who can’t tolerate the drugs;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.560
so, no wonder they see
such low rates

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.680
in the actual trials where
all those folks who suffered

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.960
never made it into the trials
in the first place.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.120
Furthermore, the majority
of clinical trials

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:26.880
don’t systematically ask

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:28.960
if people are experiencing
side effects,

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:32.880
since the trials are often run
by the company that makes them;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.720
so, there’s an incentive
to "don’t ask, don’t tell."

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.040
Observational studies
out in the real world,

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:39.920
on the other hand,

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.480
don’t find muscle symptoms
in one in a thousand,

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:45.520
but rather one in ten
or even one in five,

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.880
that’s one or two hundred
times higher rates

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:50.800
than seen in the trials.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:52.400
Some have attributed
this difference

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.040
between observational
and experimental studies

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:58.000
to the nocebo effect,

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.360
where patients experience
side effects they are expecting,

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:04.240
but it’s really
all in their head.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:08.640
Or it could be because nearly
all trials never actually asked,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.240
and even when they did ask,

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.440
industry-sponsored trials
are known to underreport,

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.040
known to sweep adverse effects
under the rug.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.640
Finally, statin trials
have often excluded patients

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:22.080
with a history
of muscle problems,

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.880
who may be in the population
most at risk.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.520
How do we get to the bottom
of this?

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:29.920
Well, if you
rechallenge patients

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:32.880
who claim
to be statin intolerant

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:34.720
with the real thing
or a placebo,

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:37.360
you can see what proportion
have muscle aches

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.760
that are actually caused
by the drugs,

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.440
and when you do that,

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:46.000
between 1/3 and 1/2 of patients
consistently report muscle pain

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:48.240
on statins but not placebo.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:49.760
So, yes, a lot of people

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.600
who think statins
are causing their muscle pains

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.120
are actually mistaken,

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:54.080
but, at the same time,

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:56.320
a lot of people
really are suffering

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:58.000
because of these drugs.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.240
The European
Atherosclerosis Society

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.520
estimates the overall risk

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:04.640
of developing
statin-related muscle symptoms

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.360
to be anywhere from 1 in 14
who go on statins

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:10.400
to nearly one in three.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.600
Most trials
also don’t systematically ask

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:16.880
about other potential
adverse effects like fatigue,

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:17.920
even though fatigue

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:20.240
is among the most commonly
reported problems

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:22.000
by patients on statins.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.720
In a non-industry-funded trial

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.120
on the effects of statins
versus placebos,

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.760
on energy and fatigue
with exertion,

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:33.840
effectively 4 out of 10
treated women

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.320
cited worsening
in either energy levels

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.400
or exertional fatigue.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:40.480
What about effects
on the brain?

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.520
In 2012, the U.S. Food
and Drug Administration

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:46.640
issued a new warning
for the labeling of statin drugs

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:49.760
regarding potential
adverse effects on cognition,

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:52.640
including memory loss
and confusion.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.280
That was based
on early studies like these,

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.440
but, thankfully,
more recent evidence

00:04:57.440 --> 00:04:59.360
does not strongly support
a link

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.840
between statins
and cognitive impairment.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:05.280
By 2015, putting all
the relevant studies together,

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.760
a systematic review
concluded that statin therapy

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:09.360
did not appear
to be associated

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.440
with cognitive impairment,

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:13.920
but again,
that’s from clinical trials.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:16.960
What about statin use
and dementia risk?

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:17.920
Dozens of studies,

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:20.880
involving a total of more
than seven million patients,

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:23.760
found that statin use
was linked to lower risks

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:26.320
of dementias,
like Alzheimer’s disease.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:28.480
This could be because
of the role cholesterol plays

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:30.080
in the development
of Alzheimer’s,

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:32.000
as I’ve explored before,

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:35.280
but it also could be due to
things like "healthy user bias,"

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.760
where people
who diligently take statins

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.560
may also engage in other
health-promoting behaviors,

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:44.080
and it’s those behaviors
that reduce the risk

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:46.320
rather than the drugs
themselves.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:47.600
In the Alzheimer’s video,

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:52.960
I profiled Dr. Dean Ornish’s
landmark 2024 study

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:54.640
showing that a whole food
plant-based diet

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:56.240
and other
healthy lifestyle behaviors

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:58.320
could apparently reverse
the progression

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.800
of early-stage
Alzheimer’s disease

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:02.320
similar to his previous work

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:04.880
showing that a similar
plant-based diet and lifestyle

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:08.000
could reverse the progression
of heart disease,

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:11.120
but it doesn’t have to be
all or nothing.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:13.760
In the Lyon Diet Heart Study,

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:16.160
heart attack survivors
were randomized to advice

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:18.640
to eat a more
Mediterranean-style diet

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:21.200
rich in plant-based omega-3s

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:22.800
or to just continue to eat

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.120
whatever their doctors
told them to eat.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:29.520
And the dietary advice group
ate more bread, more fruit,

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:31.920
less ham and sausage
and other meat,

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:33.440
less butter and cream,

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.280
and though they didn’t
significantly change

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:36.720
their fish consumption,

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.000
they did eat more
of a provided butter-less spread

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.000
enriched with
plant-based omega-3s,

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:44.800
like the kind found
in flaxseeds and walnuts.

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:48.480
And check out
those survival curves.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:49.680
Over the next five years,

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:52.560
only about 5% of the people
in the diet group

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.800
suffered a fatal
or nonfatal heart attack,

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:58.560
compared to more than 20%
of the control group.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:02.240
Health professionals,
the pharmaceutical industries,

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:04.560
and research funding
and regulatory agencies

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:06.400
are almost totally focused

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.440
on lowering cholesterol levels
using drugs,

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:12.400
so it’s heartening to see
a well-conducted study

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:15.040
find that relatively simple
dietary changes

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:18.000
achieved greater reductions

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:19.680
in the risk
of all-cause mortality

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:20.800
and heart disease mortality

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:22.240
in a secondary prevention trial

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.440
than any of the drug studies
to date.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:29.760
Diet beat out drugs
when it came to death.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:31.440
Despite the striking findings,

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:34.800
we’re talking 70% reduction
in the risk of premature death

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:36.080
from just eating
more plant foods

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:37.520
and less meat and dairy.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:40.720
Few cardiologists
may even be aware of the study,

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:41.520
even though it was published

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:43.840
in one of the most prestigious
medical journals

00:07:43.840 --> 00:07:46.954
in the world.

